# 🔵 1. TCP — Transmission Control Protocol

TCP is a connection-oriented transport protocol.

It provides mechanisms for:

* Reliable delivery
* Ordered data transfer
* Acknowledgements
* Retransmission
* Flow control

### TCP Three-Way Handshake

A TCP connection begins with:

```text
Client                    Server

  SYN  -------------------->
       <---------------- SYN + ACK
  ACK  -------------------->
```

After this process, the TCP connection can exchange application data.

---

# 🟢 2. UDP — User Datagram Protocol

UDP is a connectionless transport protocol.

It has less protocol overhead than TCP and does not provide TCP-style reliability, ordering, or retransmission.

Common uses include:

* DNS
* DHCP
* VoIP
* Streaming
* Online gaming

---

# 🔍 TCP vs UDP

| TCP                          | UDP                           |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Connection-oriented          | Connectionless                |
| Reliable delivery mechanisms | No TCP-style reliability      |
| Ordered byte stream          | Datagram-based                |
| More overhead                | Lower overhead                |
| Uses acknowledgements        | No TCP-style acknowledgements |
| Example: HTTPS               | Example: DNS                  |

---

# 🔢 3. Network Ports

A port helps identify a transport-layer service endpoint.

Example:

```text
192.168.1.10:443
```

Here:

```text
192.168.1.10 → IP address
443           → Port
```

