# 💻 Practical Exercise 1 — Check Network Connections

On Windows, I used:

```cmd
netstat -ano
```

This command can display:

* Local addresses
* Local ports
* Remote addresses
* Remote ports
* Connection state
* Process ID (PID)

Example:

```text
TCP    192.168.1.10:51524    142.250.x.x:443    ESTABLISHED
```

This can be interpreted as a TCP connection from a local ephemeral port to a remote HTTPS service.

---

# 💻 Practical Exercise 2 — Find Listening Ports

I used:

```cmd
netstat -ano | findstr LISTENING
```

This helped me identify services that are listening for incoming TCP connections.

### Linux

Equivalent commands include:

```bash
ss -tuln
```

or:

```bash
ss -tulpn
```

